n has no material. I will not fabricate paths or code facts.\n\nWhat I can carry instead (clearly labeled as *public-documentation knowledge + the task's own hints*, to be re-verified against the real source at design-finalization time):\n- The flag is `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward`, in the Megatron-LM \"zero-bubble\" pipeline scheduling family (`megatron/core/pipeline_parallel/schedules.py`, args in `megatron/arguments.py`), which implements the Zero-Bubble line of schedules (ZB-H2 \"V-shape\", ZB-1P / ZB-2P \"pass\"-based schedules). The task hint says ZB-2p is activated by setting the flag to **2 \u00d7 (number of pipeline stages)**, where \"pipeline stages\" = the pipeline-parallel degree (PP / the `--num-layers-per-stage`-derived stage count). The assumption behind a large max-pending-backward: it bounds how many backward steps can be \"pending\" so forward steps of later micro-batches overlap backward work, which is precisely what fills the bubble in 2-pass schedules.\n- Key assumptions this scheduling algorithm typically makes (public knowledge; to confirm against real source): (a) **uniform/homogeneous stage compute times** (bubble-free only when every stage's forward+backward take equal time; heterogeneity re-creates bubble), (b) **backward-bubble overlap requires enough micro-batches in flight** (M must be \u2273 2\u00d7P for a 2-pass schedule to saturate), (c) **memory** \u2014 2-pass holds more activations/state in flight than 1-pass, so the schedule's feasibility on a given model+stage is memory-bound, (d) the bubble it provably removes is the *steady-state* bubble, not the ramp-up/drain transient, and (e) p2p communication overlaps with compute (no communication-only stall counted as bubble if overlapped).\n\n### 2. Proposed experiment-design skeleton\n**Bubble definition (proposed, to be agreed):** Per pipeline stage, for a *steady-state window* in the middle of an iteration (excluding micro-batch ramp-up and final drain), bubble time = wall time during which the stage's GPU is idle (no forward/backward compute kernels and no required p2p/collective comm executing), while other stages are still progressing. Aggregates: stage-level bubble rate = bubble_time / window_duration; headline metric = mean over stages, with max-over-stages reported (max is the scheduler-relevant bound).\n\n**Profiling methodology (two complementary layers):**\n- *Scheduler-level (deterministic):* instrument the emitted ZB-2p event stream (stage, micro-batch, fwd/bwd, comm) to derive the theoretical steady-state timeline and the ideal bubble from the schedule itself; this separates \"schedule is supposed to be bubble-free\" from \"execution realized it\".\n- *Execution-level (empirical):* per-GPU kernel timeline via NVIDIA Nsight Systems (`nsys`) and/or `torch.profiler` traces collected on non-timing iterations (guard against profiler perturbation), classifying GPU intervals into compute (fwd/bwd), comm (p2p/collectives via their kernel names), and idle. Idle fraction inside the steady-state window = empirical bubble rate.\n\n**Target configurations:**\n- Pipeline stage counts P \u2208 {4, 8} (and P=16 if budget allows), homogeneous stage sizes; world sizes e.g. (PP,DP): (4,4)=16, (8,2)=16, (4,8)=32, (8,4)=32 \u2014 so we vary both P and stage count on a fixed total GPU budget.\n- Workload: decoder-only GPT-style transformer (standard for Zero-Bubble papers) with a config that must leave memory headroom for 2-pass in-flight state at M = 2P; plus one deliberately **non-uniform** stage workload (different per-stage layer counts) as a control against the homogeneity assumption.\n- Micro-batch sweep: M \u2208 {P, 2P, 4P, 8P} micro-batches (M=2P is the minimum for the 2P in-flight pending backward; M/P ratios above probe saturation). Global batch = M \u00d7 DP.\n\n**Controls / baselines:**\n- Same model+cluster under the default non-zero-bubble schedule (1F1B, and interleaved VPP if available) \u2192 absolute-reference bubble rate.\n- ZB-1P / ZB-H2 variants when available \u2192 isolate what 2-pass specifically buys.\n- Sweep `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward` \u2208 {P, 2P, 4P} at fixed M \u2192 confirm 2\u00d7P is the saturating/prescribed value (a \"flag-adequacy\" control).\n- Uniform vs non-uniform stages; and an empirical idle-noise floor control (stage forced busy) to set the detection floor for \"zero\".\n\n**Trials/repetitions:** \u2265 5 (10 if variance high) repetitions per (P, DP, M, schedule, max-pending) cell, randomized run order across cells to decorrelate cluster drift/thermal state; report mean \u00b1 std and medians; normalize timings per-run (e.g., use same data/batch shapes and record achieved FLOPS and clock throttling) so heterogeneity across GPU IDs does not masquerade as schedule behavior.\n\n**Analysis plan (deciding \"near zero\"):**\n- Pre-register operational threshold: **steady-state residual bubble \u2264 2% of steady-state window** is \"near zero\" in practice (proposed; pending agreement \u2014 see assumptions).\n- Primary test: for each P/DP cell, one-sided upper confidence bound on the mean bubble rate (bootstrap or t-based) must sit below the threshold to claim \"near zero\".\n- Secondary: paired per-run comparison of ZB-2p vs 1F1B baseline (same cell) via Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-run bubble rates; report effect size.\n- Decomposition of any residual bubble into: ramp/drain transient share, memory-stall (max-pending saturation) share, comm-imbalance share, stage-compute-imbalance share \u2014 to attribute *why* near-zero holds or fails.\n- Sensitivity curves: bubble_rate vs M/P, vs P, vs max-pending; plus a sanity check that the scheduler-level theoretical bubble \u2248 empirical bubble (validates the measurement, not the claim).\n\n**Feasibility:** No GPUs here (`gpu_enabled:false`, network disabled) \u2014 the design targets an external multi-GPU cluster (e.g., 16\u201332\u00d7 A100/H100); specify software stack (Megatron-LM build with zero-bubble schedules, PyTorch, CUDA, Nsight Systems) as a pinned feasibility section. Budget: total \u2248 (#cells) \u00d7 (trials) \u00d7 (iteration time), to be finalized after we agree on the cell list and per-iteration wall-clock budget.\n\n### 3. Assumptions / uncertainties to settle before finalizing\n1. **Repository absence:** treated as fixed fact \u2014 no code paths can be cited; the flag and schedule semantics are taken from the task hint + public docs and must be re-verified against the real Megatron source outside this container before running.\n2. **\"Near-zero\" threshold:** proposed \u2264 2% steady-state bubble; open to your preference (1% vs 5% changes the statistical power needed).\n3. **Which profiler signal counts as bubble:** proposed = GPU idle in steady state (neither compute nor comm kernels); the alternative (only compute-idle, ignoring comm) would measure a different thing \u2014 needs your sign-off.\n4. **Stage counting for \"2\u00d7 stages\":** assumed = PP degree (number of pipeline-parallel stages). Must match the actual Megatron semantics.\n5. **GPU heterogeneity across runs / run-to-run variance:** handled via repeats + normalization; assumed variance is small enough that 5\u201310 runs give a \u2264 1% CI.\n6. **Missing compute/runtime budget:** exact GPU count and wall-clock hours unknown \u2014 I'll structure cells so they can be pruned (mandatory core matrix + optional extensions) once a budget exists.\n7. **\"Automatic scheduling algorithm\":** assumed to be Megatron's zero-bubble schedule module (the thing that emits ZB-2p when the flag is set) rather than an external auto-parallel planner; if the actual target differs, the instrumentation layer in (2) changes.\n\nRequested next step before final design: confirm the threshold, the bubble signal definition, the stage-count semantics, and an approximate GPU/runtime budget so I can pin the cell matrix and the repetition count.", "evidence": "Executed `find` over `/workspace`, `/`, `/home`, `/tmp`, `